[Music]  75 years and five days ago it's a  wonderful life premiered on the big  screen just in time for christmas the  film would earn five oscar nominations  but surprisingly wasn't considered a  commercial success yet over the decades  with a big boost from television it's a  wonderful life would grow into a  timeless classic one that still  resonates during this wonderful time of  year  a movie only stands the test of time by  capturing the hearts of nearly any  audience even a clear-eyed movie critic  like david edelstein  i was re-watching this film last night  i was kind of laughing at it and kind of  rolling my eyes  and then all of a sudden i noticed my  eyes were wet  and then i noticed that tears were  coming down my face i was like what what  has happened  what is happening to me  the same thing that's been happening for  the last 75 years to so many watching  jimmy stewart hey nick nick isn't that  mr goddard  in the frank capra classic it's a  wonderful life  i think the combination of capra's  camera of jimmy stewart's elation of how  deep and dark it has gotten and then the  joy at the end who could resist that  it's magical  for the uninitiated the film takes place  in the fictional small town of bedford  falls  following the life of george bailey who  gives up his own dreams to help others  even as a child he has big dreams he  wants to build cities he wants to become  a master of the universe i want to do  something big and something important  and again and again we will see george  forest doctor let's get this thing  straight i'm leaving i'm leaving right  now i'm going to school this is my last  chance following his better instincts  but  against his will  to put others before him right mary i  know what i'm gonna do tomorrow and the  next day and next year and a year after  that i'm shaking the dust of this crummy  little town off my feet and i'm gonna  see the world  italy greece the park this crummy little  town distraught one christmas eve bayley  questions whether his life has mattered  at all that's when a guardian angel  steps in you just don't know all that  you've done if it hadn't been for you  yeah if it hadn't been for me  everybody'd be a lot better off it's a  very american story what is success  how do you define it and if you don't  become rich and famous does that mean  you're nothing or nobody film historian  janine basinger is the author of the  definitive book on it's a wonderful life  this is fantastic i love this and you  see the meticulous  planning and the keeper of director  frank capra's archives was there  anything that stood out that surprised  you when it came to the movie and frank  capra a lot of things surprised me the  main surprise i think is that it was  never intended to be a christmas movie  because he was always aware that this  movie has a real  downer section and it just didn't feel  like a christmas movie in that regard  think about that now exactly it's on  every christmas exactly it was turned  into a christmas movie of course by  television was frank aware of what it  became frank was more than aware of what  it became because as he once said to me  i got a letter from somebody who saw it  on tv  i sat down in a chair to write an answer  to that letter it's been 20 years now  janine and i'm still in that chair  writing letters and he said it was  exactly that  [Music]  the film was shot in los angeles and  capra never revealed the inspiration for  bedford falls  but some believe signs point to seneca  falls new york which has laid claim to  the distinction  there you'll find the it's a wonderful  life museum  and for almost two decades seneca falls  has transformed itself into bedford  falls hosting an annual festival for the  faithful  earlier this month surviving actors of  the movie gathered there  [Music]  including the bayley kids do you have  memories of that set of making this  movie  oh yeah it was the first time i'd ever  seen snow it wasn't real right you know  i thought it was pretty cool  carolyn grimes was just six when she  played zuzia the youngest daughter of  george and mary bailey and delivers one  of the film's unforgettable lines  [Music]  what is it like when you meet these  different fans oh my gosh their face  lights up i mean it's like i'm some kind  of an angel from heaven yeah  always the little girl they see when  they see me so you know she was beloved  and her name is named after you  that just shows in their faces and in  their hearts i had always heard the  reference about when a bell rings and  angel gets its wings right  but that's all i knew about it last year  ashley burton watched the film for her  millennial movie monday youtube series  and see if they stood the test of time  reacting in real time i wish i had a  million dollars  hot dog  what why not right here ah detention  before giving her review yes sir kiss  her from a millennial standpoint of  course it's in black and white and i was  like oh lord this is  we throwing it way back but for the most  part it's a great story about family  that even on the days where you feel  like you maybe don't matter or you  didn't make any big decisions  your life adds up and means something  each man's life touches so many other  lives  thesis statement to david edelstein the  redemption in the last 40 minutes isn't  just for george bailey have returns  please it's also for the movie please  god let me know and on the word god the  snow begins it's not his sadness that  makes you cry it's his joy jenny tommy  and who could resist this  [Applause]  and the last line of the film the last  spoken line is attaboy clarence who has  gotten his wings  and all is right  in the world  and the world on high  a feeling of hope that all can be right  in the world bringing us back year after  year  i think really the last part is what  assures this film a place in the history  of not just christmas movies but  american movies  and why people  75 years from now may still be watching  it it's why people see this film again  and again in  in dark times and in bright times  [Music]  [Music]

